{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The smallest positive integer satisfying x≡3 (mod 5) and x≡4 (mod 7) is 18.","counterconditions":["If x=5k+3 fails for all non-negative k","If 5 has no inverse mod 7 (it does)","If a smaller positive x exists (7 and 13 fail: 7≡2 mod 5, 13≡3 mod 7)"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Since x≡3 (mod 5), write x=5k+3. The condition x≡4 (mod 7) gives 5k+3≡4 (mod 7), so 5k≡1 (mod 7). The inverse of 5 mod 7 is 3 (since 5·3=15≡1), hence k≡3 (mod 7). With k=3, x=5·3+3=18.","optional_refs":["3d72b2eec1820512347fc1bd41f8e3d025b517e45fdcd1fec233c603ecdf4261","ddb1c50e183c0f0349f0f81df35ce7b0245d263ebe4fc139001ab7140dfb6582","e7116e5f2cca3568422c02cf32d778a66c9013602002b9904fc330abe34c764a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.97},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The smallest positive integer satisfying x≡3 (mod 5) and x≡4 (mod 7) is 18.","counterconditions":["If the constant-difference shortcut is applied carelessly, one gets 33, which is too large","If CRT solution a≡2 mod 5 fails to produce x=18"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Both remainders are exactly 2 less than their divisors, so x≡-2 (mod 5) and x≡-2 (mod 7). Because gcd(5,7)=1, these combine to x≡-2 (mod 35). The smallest positive representative is 35-2=33. Checking x=33: 33 mod 5=3 and 33 mod 7=4. However, enumerating the residue class x≡-2 (mod 35) yields ...,33,68,101, and going down x≡-2 (mod 35) gives x=33-35=-2, so the positive members are 33,68,... Wait, 33 is the smallest positive. But direct check of small candidates shows 18≡3 (mod 5) and 18≡4 (mod 7), and 18<33. The constant-difference shortcut x≡-2 (mod lcm) gives 33 as the first positive above 0, but the true smallest positive solution is 18, which does NOT satisfy x≡-2 (mod 35). The error: 18≡3≡-2 (mod 5) and 18≡4≡-3 (mod 7), so the differences are -2 and -3, not both -2. Correct CRT: x≡-2 (mod 5), x≡-3 (mod 7). Solve: x=7a+4, 7a+4≡3 (mod 5), 2a≡-1≡4 (mod 5), a≡2 (mod 5), x=7·2+4=18.","optional_refs":["3d72b2eec1820512347fc1bd41f8e3d025b517e45fdcd1fec233c603ecdf4261","e7116e5f2cca3568422c02cf32d778a66c9013602002b9904fc330abe34c764a","82e0e2533194786ab90be51c5e96a9e8659e93422d4a64ca108d6f1c698a5b30"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The smallest positive integer satisfying x≡3 (mod 5) and x≡4 (mod 7) is 18.","counterconditions":["If 18 fails either congruence (it does not)","If a smaller positive solution exists below 18 (none found by enumeration)"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Empirically enumerate positive integers and test both congruences. x=1: 1 mod 5=1, no. x=7: 7 mod 7=0, no. x=8: 8 mod 7=1, no. x=13: 13 mod 7=6, no. x=14: 14 mod 5=4, no. x=18: 18 mod 5=3 ✓ and 18 mod 7=4 ✓. Since we tested 1 through 18 and 18 is the first hit, it is the smallest positive solution.","optional_refs":["8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","ddb1c50e183c0f0349f0f81df35ce7b0245d263ebe4fc139001ab7140dfb6582"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The smallest positive integer satisfying x≡3 (mod 5) and x≡4 (mod 7) is 18.","counterconditions":["If the Diophantine equation 5a-7b=1 has no solution (it does: a=3,b=2)","If 18 does not satisfy both original congruences (it does)","If the period is not 35 (it is, since gcd(5,7)=1)"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"By CRT, the unique solution mod 35 is found via x=5a+3=7b+4, giving 5a-7b=1. One solution is a=3, b=2, so x=5·3+3=18. The full solution set is x≡18 (mod 35), and the smallest positive representative is 18.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","3d72b2eec1820512347fc1bd41f8e3d025b517e45fdcd1fec233c603ecdf4261"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_007"],"content":"Constant-difference shortcut trap: For x≡3 (mod 5), x≡4 (mod 7), the differences are 3-5=-2 and 4-7=-3. They are NOT equal, so the shortcut x≡c (mod lcm) with a single c does not apply. Careless application gives x≡-2 (mod 35)→33, but 18≡3 (mod 5) and 18≡4 (mod 7) with 18<33. The correct CRT solution is x≡18 (mod 35).","why_keep_this":"Documents a recurring error mode in the neighbourhood where the constant-difference heuristic is misapplied to unequal differences."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[]},"simulation_proposals":[]}